WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
Work on open projects is scattered
across repositories, chats and forums.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:11.639
Anyone can claim anything, and
checking a claim means chasing people down.

00:00:11.989 --> 00:00:18.084
ProofMe turns a contribution into a record,
confirmed by the organisation that saw the work.

00:00:19.393 --> 00:00:23.203
This is Daniel Okafor, a sample contributor.

00:00:23.553 --> 00:00:28.692
His public profile keeps verified
work apart from what he reports himself.

00:00:29.042 --> 00:00:35.457
Each verified item names the organisation that
confirmed it, and links to a record anyone can check.

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:42.514
Daniel adds new work: what he did,
his role, and a link to the evidence.

00:00:42.864 --> 00:00:48.468
It starts as a private draft.
Nobody else can see it until he sends it.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:55.545
He sends it to Northstar Protocol, a
fictional organisation, to confirm.

00:00:55.895 --> 00:00:58.804
Only Northstar's reviewers will see it.

00:01:00.105 --> 00:01:07.015
Priya reviews for Northstar. The claim is waiting
in her queue, with the evidence Daniel attached.

00:01:07.365 --> 00:01:12.654
Nobody can approve their own claim, and
the server checks her role on every decision.

00:01:13.957 --> 00:01:22.252
She approves it. The claim is frozen into an
attestation: a canonical record and its SHA-256 digest.

00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:26.212
Anyone can open the public record.

00:01:26.562 --> 00:01:31.736
The page hashes the record again, to
show it has not changed since approval.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:36.715
Portable, accountable proof of contribution.

00:01:37.065 --> 00:01:41.719
Confirmed by the people who saw
the work, and checkable by anyone.
